      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "No fixed release is available yet; update when Docker Hardened Images publishes one.",
      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-3446: binascii.a2b_base64(), and so base64.b64decode() and related functions, stopped decoding at the first padded quad and silently ignored the rest of the input, so data could be accepted and read differently than by other implementations. Upstream fixed it in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/145264 (commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/4561f6418a691b3e89aef0901f53fe0dfb7f7c0e), released in CPython 3.13.13 and 3.14.4 only; see the advisory https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/F5ZT5ICGJ6CKXVUJ34YBVY7WOZ5SHG53/ and https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3446, which lists every release before 3.13.13 as affected. The fix was not backported to the 3.12 branch: Modules/binascii.c is the same file in the CPython 3.12.13, 3.12.14, 3.12.15 and 3.12.16 releases. The DHI Alpine 3.23 python-3.12 package carries no fix for it. Ran binascii.a2b_base64(b'YQ==YQ==') with the interpreter from every published build (3.12.12-r0 to 3.12.16-r4) on x86_64 and aarch64: every build stops at the first padded quad and returns b'a', the behaviour the fix removes, so every published build is affected."
    },
